      "id": "seller-20260923",
      "section": "business",
      "fly": "E-commerce",
      "title": "Amazon lets the agents in",
      "rubric": "The world’s biggest online marketplace opens its seller tools to outside AI. That is a bet on control, not surrender",
      "date": "2026-09-28",
      "eventDate": "2026-09-23",
      "editionId": "2026-09-28",
      "kind": "Analysis",
      "body": [
        "For years Amazon has preferred its merchants to work inside its walls. Seller Central, the dashboard through which millions of independent businesses manage their listings, is where prices are changed, stock is counted and advertising is bought. On September 23rd, at its Accelerate conference for sellers, Amazon said it would let outside AI agents in. A beta plug-in in America allows merchants to manage inventory, prices, listings and analytics through Anthropic’s Claude or Amazon’s own Quick assistant, without opening the dashboard at all.",
        "The reason is that sellers had already left. Amazon says about 90% of them use outside AI for some part of their operations. Many paste reports into chatbots or pay third-party software firms to automate repricing. By offering an official route, Amazon can decide which actions an agent may take and log what it does. That is better than discovering that a merchant’s home-made bot has cut every price by half overnight.",
        "The choice of partner is also telling. The Seller Assistant runs on Bedrock, Amazon’s cloud service for AI models, and combines Amazon’s Nova models with Claude. Amazon is a large investor in Anthropic. The arrangement keeps the computing, and the data about what sellers ask for, inside Amazon’s cloud, even when the conversation takes place in a rival interface. A free 12-month Quick Plus subscription for primary account holders, available until the end of the year, is the sweetener.",
        "The risks are the ones that come with any agent given a company card. Repricing is a sensitive operation; marketplaces have suffered absurd price spirals when competing algorithms react to each other. Errors in listings can breach product-safety rules. Amazon will have to decide how much an agent may change without a human clicking “confirm”, and sellers will have to decide how much they trust it to. The beta is limited to America, and the terms after the free year are not yet public.",
        "The broader trend is clear enough. Platforms that once fought scrapers are now building doors for agents, on the theory that a controlled door is better than a broken window. Whether merchants get more autonomy, or Amazon gets more visibility into how they run their businesses, will depend on the small print. ■"
      ],
