      "id": "tekever-20260923",
      "section": "markets",
      "fly": "Defence technology",
      "title": "Drones over Lisbon",
      "rubric": "A Portuguese maker of surveillance aircraft raises money at a valuation once reserved for software",
      "date": "2026-09-28",
      "eventDate": "2026-09-23",
      "editionId": "2026-09-28",
      "kind": "Analysis",
      "body": [
        "European defence used to be a sleepy business for venture capitalists. Contracts were slow, customers were few and governments preferred national champions with decades of history. That has changed. On September 23rd Bloomberg reported that Tekever, a Portuguese maker of uncrewed aircraft, had closed the first tranche of a Series D round: $580m at a valuation of $6.4bn. The round was led by UC Investments, which manages money for the University of California, and Baillie Gifford, a Scottish fund manager, with several other investors joining.",
        "Tekever is best known for long-endurance surveillance drones, aircraft that can stay aloft for many hours and send back imagery and signals. Such machines have proved their worth in the war in Ukraine, where cheap persistent observation guides artillery and spots incoming threats. What investors are buying is not only the airframe but the software that decides what to look at and what to report. Turning hours of video into a few useful alerts is where AI matters most.",
        "The size of the valuation says more about the market than about the company. A few years ago a European drone firm with a few hundred staff would have been valued as an engineering business, at a modest multiple of sales. Now it is priced like a growth company, because governments across the continent have promised to spend far more on defence and want suppliers that can deliver quickly. UC Investments called this its first direct investment in Europe. Bloomberg reported that Tekever’s founder is also looking at acquisitions in defence.",
        "The risks are equally plain. Defence budgets are political, and the companies that win early contracts are not always the ones that win production orders. Export licences can disappear when governments change their minds. And autonomy in weapons and surveillance raises legal and ethical questions that procurement officials have not finished answering. Tekever’s investors are betting that Europe’s rearmament will be long, and that software will decide who profits from it. ■"
      ],
